# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Bolt's sign-in — oauth2-proxy in front of the app builder (bolt.ontrak.technology).
#
# These are **references**, not values: every credential this proxy needs lives in
# Cerulean Vault (HashiCorp Vault, KV v2) at `cerulean/ontrak/bolt-sso`, in the estate's
# `vault://<mount>/<path>#<key>` form. `scripts/deploy.sh` resolves them into the
# environment `docker-compose.all.yml` interpolates, because oauth2-proxy is a stock
# image: its entrypoint is the proxy itself, so unlike this repo's own images there
# is nothing inside the container to resolve a reference before the config is read.
#
#   cp bolt-sso/.env.example bolt-sso/.env      # once per deployment
#
# The client is the family's own `ontrak` OIDC client — the same one ONYX's,
# G0DM0D3's and Engineer's doors use — so one sign-in covers the family and rotating
# the client in Authentik means writing the new secret into the vault path. What is
# *not* shared is the cookie secret: each door signs its own session cookie, because
# two proxies sharing one key means a cookie minted by one is accepted by the other.
#
# `bolt.ontrak.technology/oauth2/callback` has to be a redirect URI of that client
# (`scripts/cerulean-ontrak.py --print-redirect-uris` lists the set); without it the
# provider answers the sign-in with `invalid_redirect_uri` instead of a login.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ONTRAK_BOLT_SSO_CLIENT_ID=vault://cerulean/ontrak/bolt-sso#OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_ID
ONTRAK_BOLT_SSO_CLIENT_SECRET=vault://cerulean/ontrak/bolt-sso#OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET
ONTRAK_BOLT_SSO_COOKIE_SECRET=vault://cerulean/ontrak/bolt-sso#OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET
